        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          The Mir 23 crew, consisting of two Russian Space Agency (RSA) crewmembers, Commander Vasily Tsibliev and Flight Engineer Alexander Lazutkin, and European Space Agency (ESA) crewmember Reinhold Ewald from Germany, launched in a Soyuz TM-25 spacecraft in February 1997 to join the Mir 22 crew and NASA 4 crewmember aboard the Mir Space Station.  After familiarizing the Mir 23 crew with current projects and procedures, the Mir 22 crew and ESA cosmonaut returned to Earth on the Soyuz TM-24.<P>Several mishaps occurred during the Mir 23/NASA 4 mission.  In March 1997, a flash fire occurred in an experimental instrument, and mechanical problems arose with the oxygen regeneration units aboard the station.  However, the mission continued successfully.<P>In May 1997, Michael Foale, the NASA 5 crewmember, was brought to the station to replace John Blaha, the NASA 4 crewmember.   While the Shuttle (STS-84) was docked to the station, astronauts and cosmonauts transferred science samples and data collected by the Mir 22/NASA 4 crew from Mir to the Shuttle.  Crewmembers also transferred hardware and supplies to Mir in support of the health and well-being of the Mir 23/NASA 5 crew, as well as to support future investigations on the station. <P>On June 25, 1997 while the NASA 5 crewmember was still on board Mir, an unmanned Progress resupply ship collided into the Spektr Module during docking maneuvers causing an oxygen leak in the Spektr Module and severely damaging the solar arrays.  The Mir 23/NASA 5 crew sealed off the module to prevent loss of oxygen from the entire station.  However, all experiments and data that were contained within the Spektr Module were lost.<P>On August 7, 1997, the Russian Mir 24 crew arrived to replace the Mir 23 crew and perform the necessary repairs to the station. <END>
        </div>

